 ],
 "literal": "Cutting the throat of a young goat, placing offerings of millet, / at the fork of the path where rivers flow, as many musical instruments resound, / other than appearing, they do not become medicine for the sickness, / praising along with many other great deities, / being said of her, 'she is possessed by a spirit', / is indeed painful, O friend! On the great mountains / where clouds play, the lord of the land, / we who are without fault suffer this!",
 "translation": "O friend! Cutting the throat of a young goat, offering millet, and making many musical instruments resound at the fork of the path, they praise many other great deities. But these deities, other than just appearing, cannot cure her sickness. Yet, they say of her, 'She is possessed by a spirit.' This is truly painful! We, who have done no wrong, must suffer this, while the lord of the land where clouds play on the great mountains remains free.",
 "modern": "தோழி! ஆட்டுக்குட்டியின் கழுத்தை அறுத்து, தினை உணவை பலியாகப் படைத்து, வழிகள் பிரியும் சந்தியில் பல இசைக்கருவிகள் முழங்க, நோயைத் தீர்க்கும் மருந்தாகாமல் வெறும் காட்சிக்கு மட்டுமே வரும் வேறு பல பெரிய தெய்வங்களை எல்லாம் வழிபட்டு, 'இவளைப் பேய் பிடித்துவிட்டது' என்று ஊரார் கூறுவது மிகவும் வருத்தமளிக்கிறது. மேகங்கள் விளையாடும் பெரிய மலைநாட்டை உடைய தலைவனை விரும்பியதில் எந்தத் தவறும் செய்யாத நாம், இந்தத் துன்பத்தை அனுபவிக்க வேண்டியுள்ளதே!",
 "explanation": "In this Kuriñci poem, the friend (tōḻi) speaks to the heroine about the uselessness of the exorcism ritual (Veriyāṭṭu) arranged by her family. The foster-mother, unaware of the heroine's secret love for the mountain lord, believes her physical decline is caused by a spirit and arranges a sacrifice. The friend laments that they are sacrificing a young goat and offering millet to external deities who can only 'appear' through the priest's trance but cannot cure the sickness of love. The image of clouds playing on the great mountains of the hero's land contrasts the hero's freedom with the heroine's confinement and suffering. The key words 'piḻaiyēm' (we who are blameless) and 'nōtakkaṉṟu' (it is painful) highlight the injustice of their situation.",
